public class LongestCommonSubstring {

    public static String findLongestCommonSubstring(String[] strs) {
        if (strs == null || strs.length == 0) {
            return "";
        }

        // Step 1: Find the shortest string to minimize substring generation
        String shortest = strs[0];
        for (String str : strs) {
            if (str.length() < shortest.length()) {
                shortest = str;
            }
        }

        int m = shortest.length();

        // Step 2: Try substring lengths from maximum possible down to 1
        for (int len = m; len > 0; len--) {
            // Slide a window of size 'len' across the shortest string
            for (int start = 0; start <= m - len; start++) {
                String candidate = shortest.substring(start, start + len);

                // Step 3: Check if candidate exists in all strings
                boolean isCommon = true;
                for (String str : strs) {
                    if (!str.contains(candidate)) {
                        isCommon = false;
                        break; // Stop checking this candidate if any string misses it
                    }
                }

                // First valid candidate found is guaranteed to be the longest!
                if (isCommon) {
                    return candidate;
                }
            }
        }

        return "";
    }
}

Time Complexity: $O(M^2 \cdot K \cdot N)$, where $M$ is the length of the shortest string, $K$ is the number 
of strings, and $N$ is the average length of the strings (matching substrings using contains() takes $O(N)$ time)
.Space Complexity: $O(M)$ to store the temporary candidate substring window.


public class Solution {
    public static String findLongestCommonSubstring(String[] strs) {
        if (strs == null || strs.length == 0) return "";
        
        // Find shortest string to bound search range
        String ref = strs[0];
        for (String s : strs) if (s.length() < ref.length()) ref = s;
        
        int low = 1, high = ref.length();
        String ans = "";
        
        // Binary Search on Substring Length -> O(log M)
        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            String match = getCommonSub(strs, ref, mid);
            
            if (match != null) {
                ans = match;   // Found valid substring, try longer
                low = mid + 1;
            } else {
                high = mid - 1; // Not found, try shorter
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }

    private static String getCommonSub(String[] strs, String ref, int len) {
        for (int i = 0; i <= ref.length() - len; i++) {
            String sub = ref.substring(i, i + len);
            boolean present = true;
            for (String s : strs) {
                if (!s.contains(sub)) { present = false; break; }
            }
            if (present) return sub;
        }
        return null;
    }
}

Time Complexity: $O(N \cdot M \log M)$, where $M$ is the length of the shortest string and $N$ is the total
 length of all strings combined.Space Complexity: $O(M)$ to store the temporary candidate substring.


public class Solution {
    public static String isSubsequence(String str1, String str2) {
        int i = 0; // Pointer for str1 (target string)
        int j = 0; // Pointer for str2 (source string)
        
        int n1 = str1.length();
        int n2 = str2.length();
        
        // Scan str2 until we exhaust either str1 or str2
        while (i < n1 && j < n2) {
            if (str1.charAt(i) == str2.charAt(j)) {
                i++; // Target character matched! Look for the next one
            }
            j++; // Always advance in str2
        }
        
        // If i reached n1, all characters of str1 were matched in order
        return (i == n1) ? "YES" : "NO";
    }
}

Time Complexity: $O(N)$, where $N$ is the length of str2, because pointer j traverses str2 at most once in a 
single linear scan.Space Complexity: $O(1)$, because we only use two primitive integer variables (i and j) to
 track indices.